                  "Source"
                ],
                "tool tips": {
                  "Phenotype ID": "Phenotype identifier from the Association to Function Knowledge Portal, Orphanet, or the GWAS Catalog. See the Searchable options page to map identifiers to descriptive terms.",
                  "Group": "Origin of the human phenotype in each row. The GWAS Catalog (gcat_trait) and Association to Function Knowledge Portal (portal) include common traits or phenotypes, and Orphanet (rare_v2) includes rare disease phenotypes.",
                  "Phenotype": "Human phenotype associated with the gene set in each row.",
                  "Joint effect": "The joint effect of a gene set measures its relevance to the phenotype when considered jointly across all gene sets in the model; this measure quantifies whether a gene set is maximally predictive of a phenotype compared to all other gene sets in the model. All gene set effects are expressed in units of log-odds of probabilities. Consider the following thresholds for interpreting joint gene set effects: >0.01 = significant, >0.1 = strongly significant, and >1.0 = among the strongest for any phenotype and any gene set. Genes with high marginal effects but weak joint effects are relevant to the phenotype in question but likely correlated with another gene set(s) that are more relevant to the phenotype.",
                  "Marginal effect": "The marginal effect of a gene set measures its relevance to the phenotype when considered in isolation; this is the default measure by which the table is sorted and is the analog of traditional enrichment statistics. All gene set effects are expressed in units of log-odds of probabilities. Consider the following thresholds for interpreting marginal gene set effects: >0.01 = significant, >0.1 = strongly significant, and >1.0 = among the strongest for any phenotype and any gene set.",
